# Backend API base URL.
#
# DEV (`expo start` / dev client): leave unset. src/services/api.ts
# auto-detects your dev machine from the Metro host, so it keeps working when
# you switch Wi-Fi networks. (Backend must run with --host 0.0.0.0 — see
# ../ANDROID_BUILD.md.)
#
# RELEASE / PREVIEW APK: REQUIRED. An installed app has no dev server to
# auto-detect, and this value is inlined into the JS bundle at BUILD time —
# changing it means rebuilding the APK. EAS builds refuse to start without it.
#   - Tunnel (recommended for testing): the https URL printed by
#       `ngrok http 8000`  ->  EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://abc123.ngrok-free.app
#   - Same Wi-Fi: your PC's LAN IP  ->  EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://192.168.1.23:8000
#     (plain http:// is allowed automatically for that build; https:// is not weakened)
#   - Deployed server: https://api.example.com
#
# For EAS cloud builds this file is NOT uploaded (it's gitignored) — set the
# value in the EAS environment instead:
#   npx eas-cli env:create --environment preview --name EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL \
#       --value https://abc123.ngrok-free.app --visibility plaintext
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://192.168.1.23:8000

# Google Maps API key — REQUIRED for the Map screen on both platforms
# (mobile native maps via android.config.googleMaps.apiKey, and the real
# Google Maps JavaScript API on web — see WebGoogleMap.tsx). Without it the
# map falls back to a visible "Could not load Google Maps" error state.
#
# Get one from Google Cloud Console (console.cloud.google.com):
#   1. Create/select a project, enable "Maps SDK for Android" and
#      "Maps JavaScript API".
#   2. Create an API key under APIs & Services > Credentials.
#   3. For a native build, the key only takes effect after `expo prebuild`
#      / a new EAS build — Expo Go substitutes its own key and ignores this
#      entirely, so changes here won't show up until you rebuild.
#   4. If you restrict the key to Android apps (you should), the restriction
#      must list package `com.seasarathi.app` AND the SHA-1 of the key the APK
#      is SIGNED with. EAS generates its own keystore, so its SHA-1 differs from
#      your local debug keystore: get it with `npx eas-cli credentials` and add
#      it. A mismatch shows up as a blank grey map in the installed APK while
#      everything else works.
#
# For EAS builds:
#   npx eas-cli env:create --environment preview --name EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY \
#       --value <key> --visibility plaintext
# (EXPO_PUBLIC_* values are embedded in the app and readable from the APK — they
#  can never be secret, so restrict the key in Google Cloud instead.)
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=your_key_here
